<GuideArticleLayout
  title="DIM weight without the carrier guesswork"
  description="Understand what dimensional weight measures, why external carton dimensions matter, and what Packmetry deliberately does not assume."
  category="Dimensional weight"
  readTime="7 min read"
>
  <h2>DIM weight describes package size as a weight</h2>

  <p>
    Dimensional weight converts the outside volume of a
    package into a weight value using a divisor. It exists
    because a large, light carton can consume substantial
    transport space even when its physical mass is low.
  </p>

  <p>
    Packmetry keeps that calculation separate from packing
    geometry. Items are still placed using the carton’s
    internal dimensions. DIM analysis uses the carton’s
    <strong>external dimensions</strong>.
  </p>

  <div class="pm-guide-note">
    <strong>No hidden carrier default</strong>
    <p>
      Packmetry does not assume a universal DIM divisor.
      Enter the divisor and units required by the carrier or
      workflow you are actually evaluating.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why external dimensions are required</h2>

  <p>
    Dimensional weight describes the space occupied by the
    package from the outside. Falling back to internal
    dimensions would make the value look precise while
    measuring the wrong geometry.
  </p>

  <p>
    For that reason, if DIM analysis is enabled, Packmetry
    expects external length, width and height for the cartons
    involved in that analysis.
  </p>

  <h2>Actual weight and DIM weight are different signals</h2>

  <p>
    Physical gross weight comes from the packed contents plus
    carton tare when those values are known. DIM weight comes
    from external package volume and the configured divisor.
    Neither value should silently stand in for the other.
  </p>

  <p>
    When both are known, Packmetry can show an estimated
    chargeable weight as the greater of actual gross weight
    and DIM weight. That is intentionally described as an
    estimate rather than a shipping quote.
  </p>

  <h2>Packmetry is not a carrier-rate engine</h2>

  <p>
    Core DIM calculations do not apply carrier-specific
    billing rounding, service-level pricing, zone tables,
    surcharges or live rates. Those rules vary by carrier,
    service and contract and belong to a different layer of
    the product.
  </p>

  <p>
    This boundary is useful: you can compare packing plans
    on dimensional-weight behavior without pretending that
    the result is a final shipping price.
  </p>

  <h2>When “Lowest DIM weight” is useful</h2>

  <p>
    The Business workspace can rank verified candidates by
    total dimensional weight when the required DIM settings
    and external carton dimensions are available. This can
    surface a different recommendation than simply choosing
    the fewest cartons or the least empty internal volume.
  </p>

  <p>
    The important part is that the objective ranks verified
    packing candidates; it does not change the underlying
    physical fit rules.
  </p>
</GuideArticleLayout>
